178	184,500,311,984	178. Trumpet-Honeysuckle.	A single flower of Trumpet-Honeysuckle: a long narrow tube, widening gradually upwards to a short five-lobed border, with the stamens standing out of its mouth.
179	999,1183,1272,1694	Fig. 179.	A Harebell flower on its stalk: a bell-shaped corolla, wide for its length, with five short pointed lobes, the narrow calyx-teeth at its base.
180	1289,1180,1564,1692 - 1525,1368,1564,1700 - 1480,1420,1525,1700	Fig. 180.	A Phlox flower: a slender tube, with the calyx at its foot, spreading suddenly into a flat border of five rounded lobes.
181	1477,1367,1705,1699 - 1477,1367,1525,1420	Fig. 181.	A Dead-Nettle flower from the side: a two-lipped corolla, the upper lip arched like a hood over the lower one, above a small toothed calyx.
182	144,1917,377,2152	Fig. 182.	A Potato-blossom: a wheel-shaped corolla of five pointed lobes with a very short tube, the stamens gathered in a cone at the centre.
183	380,1934,700,2153	Fig. 183.	A Nightshade flower: a flat, star-shaped corolla with almost no tube, the stamens joined round the style in the centre.
